    "description": "Housing construction statistics describe different stages and dimensions of building activity, including permits, planned dwellings, floor area and completed buildings. In Iran, these distinctions are essential when interpreting claims about new housing supply.",
    "tags": ["Housing construction", "Building permits", "Urban development", "Housing supply", "Construction statistics"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T13:29:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T13:29:22Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Housing Construction in Iran: Permits and Housing Supply",
      "description": "Distinguish building permits, planned dwellings and completed housing when interpreting Iran's construction statistics."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "what-is-counted",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A permit is not a completed home",
        "content": "Housing supply cannot be understood from a single construction headline. Syracuse University's Iran Data Portal separates permits by building use, construction materials, storeys and floor area. It also distinguishes residential-building permits from the number of housing units specified in permits. These categories describe different things: a permit may cover a building containing several homes, while floor area measures physical scale rather than the number of households that can be accommodated. [s1]\n\nA count of authorizations therefore cannot establish how many dwellings were completed or occupied. Similarly, an increase in dwellings specified per permit could reflect a change in project size rather than more separate building projects. The archive's categories provide a useful framework for reading contemporary releases without treating all construction measures as interchangeable. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "annual-permits",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Building permits issued in Iran's urban areas, Solar Hijri 1394–1403",
          "dataset_id": "urban-building-permits",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["permits"],
          "x_axis_label": "Solar Hijri reporting year",
          "y_axis_label": "Building permits, count",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "Within the reproduced series, the highest count occurs in 1399. Counts subsequently fall and remain below that peak through 1403. These are building permits for urban areas, not completed dwellings or a residential-only construction total. The original statistical release still requires verification."
        },
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "construction-stages",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Following activity through its stages",
        "content": "The historical archive separately lists characteristics of buildings started and completed by private developers, alongside investment classified by construction phase. A sound account of housing delivery needs this distinction between authorization, work in progress and completion. Investment totals also answer a different question from physical output: money spent is not itself a count of finished homes. [s3]\n\nThe annual permit series presented here comes from an inspected secondary reproduction attributing its figures to the Statistical Centre of Iran. Its table covers building permits across urban areas, and does not label the count as residential-only. Consequently, it is retained as contextual evidence rather than presented as a complete housing-production series. The reproduction does not provide sufficient methodological detail to establish whether classifications or coverage changed. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "geography-and-delivery",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a national total leaves unresolved",
        "content": "For international readers, the next useful step is to connect construction measures to geography and delivery. A national count cannot reveal which cities gained usable homes, how much existing housing was replaced, or whether new development received services. The archive's separate categories for residential units, building characteristics and construction phases show why these questions need distinct evidence. [s1][s3]\n\nThis draft does not attribute changes in permits to a particular economic cause. Establishing such an explanation would require evidence on project finance, land, costs and actual building activity over matching periods. Nor does it infer current construction quality from the permit count. The defensible conclusion is narrower: authorization statistics help describe the construction pipeline, while completed and serviced housing must be measured separately.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      }
    ],
