  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-18-6-4",
    "page_path": "/explore/urban/housing/social-housing/",
    "name": "Social Housing",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/urban/housing/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Iran's social-housing debate includes subsidized access to land and homeownership as well as questions about services and affordability. Research on the Mehr programme illustrates why construction totals alone cannot establish whether housing assistance creates adequate places to live.",
    "tags": ["Social housing", "Mehr housing", "Affordable housing", "Housing policy", "Urban services"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T13:29:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T13:29:22Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Social Housing in Iran: Mehr and Housing Assistance",
      "description": "Explore Iran's housing-assistance debate through evidence on Mehr, neighbourhood effects and residents' access to services."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "meaning-and-design",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What social housing means in this context",
        "content": "Readers familiar with municipal rental housing should not assume that every Iranian programme described as social housing has the same tenure model. Research on Iran discusses assistance through land provision, subsidized finance and routes into ownership. A study of the Mehr Housing Plan describes public land and construction support as central instruments intended to reduce the cost of housing for lower-income groups. The programme's stated affordability objective must be distinguished from evidence about whom it ultimately served. [s1]\n\nThat study places Mehr within a longer history of centralized housing interventions involving land allocation, cooperatives and hire purchase. Its account shows why the label 'social housing' needs explanation: assistance can operate through the price of land or finance rather than through an enduring stock of publicly managed rental homes. These arrangements also raise different questions about eligibility, household contributions and the affordability of repayment. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "neighbourhood-effects",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Looking beyond the subsidized dwelling",
        "content": "A working paper by Saeed Tajrishy and Mohammad Vesal examines Mehr through its effects on surrounding property markets. The publisher's abstract describes comparing transaction-price changes in postal regions receiving Mehr deliveries with changes elsewhere, before and after delivery. This is an attempt to identify neighbourhood effects, rather than simply compare the listed prices of subsidized and unsubsidized homes. [s2]\n\nThe authors report lower surrounding house prices following delivery and discuss both expanded supply and possible neighbourhood disadvantages as relevant mechanisms. They also report evidence consistent with schools providing a positive local amenity. These findings should not be compressed into a claim that cheaper surrounding property proves either programme success or failure. A price change can reflect several processes, and the study does not itself provide a comprehensive measure of beneficiary welfare. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "resident-experience",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Affordability includes access",
        "content": "A separate study considers residents of Parand, a Mehr settlement in Tehran Province. Its inspected abstract describes a resident questionnaire and an analysis of residential satisfaction. The authors emphasize financial capacity and the importance of easier commuting to Tehran, arguing that urban infrastructure matters for the quality of residential life. Only the abstract was inspected, so this page does not reproduce detailed findings from the unseen article. [s3]\n\nParand's experience cannot stand for every Mehr development. It does, however, identify a question that construction counts leave unanswered: can residents reach the activities and services that make a dwelling useful? Research on programme design and research on residents' experiences address related but different parts of that question. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "assessment",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "How to assess housing assistance",
        "content": "Taken together, these sources suggest assessing assistance at several levels: the subsidy offered, the households able to participate, the housing delivered and the neighbourhood conditions after delivery. This is an editorial synthesis of the inspected research, not an official evaluation framework. It explains why land allocations, financing approvals and completed homes should not be treated as equivalent outcomes. [s1][s2][s3]\n\nA current national assessment remains unfinished. Verified eligibility rules, delivery records and evidence on the poorest households are needed before concluding how effectively present programmes meet housing needs.",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "id": "s1",
        "title": "Spatial Planning, Urban Governance and the Economic Context: The Case of ‘Mehr’ Housing Plan, Iran",
        "publisher": "MDPI, Land",
